From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: win32u: the focus never falls to a cloaked window (a window on another virtual desktop)

David (2026-11-15): "If you're on desktop 2, when you open up an
application, it opens up on desktop 1 and switches you over there."
Start (a passing tool window) went away as it started the program, and the
window Wine made active in its place was the next one in its Z order: a
window on desktop 1, shell-cloaked but still visible to Wine. The shell
took that for a window brought forward and went to desktop 1, and the
program, opening a moment later, opened there. A window that is active and
goes away now never hands the focus to a cloaked window, as on Windows,
whose virtual desktops cloak the windows of the others. A program that
brings its window on another desktop forward itself (a single-instance
program showing its window again) still takes the shell there.

Gate: test/vdopen-gate.sh (make test-vdopen): on desktop 2, with Notepad
on desktop 1, a launcher window going away leaves desktop 2 current; File
Explorer, a program started through start.exe, a framed Linux program and
a second Notepad open on desktop 2, which stays current. Mutant
SG_MUTANT_ACTIVATE_CLOAKED fails it.
diff --git a/dlls/win32u/window.c b/dlls/win32u/window.c
index 18c9880..6994fed 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/window.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/window.c
@@ -4576,6 +4576,14 @@ static BOOL can_activate_window( HWND hwnd )
     /* the system never brings a no-activate window (the taskbar) forward
      * when the window that was active goes away */
     if (get_window_long( hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE ) & WS_EX_NOACTIVATE) return FALSE;
+#endif
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_ACTIVATE_CLOAKED
+    /* Stained Glass OS: nor a cloaked one -- a window on another virtual
+     * desktop (shell-cloaked) is not where the focus falls when the active
+     * window goes: Start closing as it started a program on desktop 2 gave
+     * the foreground to a window on desktop 1, the shell went there, and the
+     * program opened there too (David 2026-11-15) */
+    if (get_window_cloaked( hwnd )) return FALSE;
 #endif
     return !(style & WS_DISABLED);
 }
